event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"To understand the internet, you must","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" first discard the notion that it is a nebulous \"cloud\" floating in the sky. In reality, the internet is","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" a massive, physical, global infrastructure of wires, glass, and silicon. It is a \"network of networks\" that","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" allows billions of devices to communicate through a standardized set of rules. To understand how a single click on your browser turns into","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" a webpage, we have to trace the journey from the physical hardware up to the software protocols.\n\n### The Physical Foundation","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":": Cables and Light\nThe journey begins with the physical layer. Most people assume the internet is primarily wireless, but the","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" vast majority of global data travels through physical cables. Underneath our oceans lie thousands of miles of fiber-optic cables—","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"thin strands of glass that transmit data as pulses of light. On land, these cables run through conduits beneath streets and alongside","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" railways.\n\nWhen you use Wi-Fi, you are only using wireless technology for the \"last few meters.\" Your","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" device sends radio waves to a router, which is physically plugged into a wall via a copper or fiber-optic cable.","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" This cable connects to your Internet Service Provider (ISP), which then routes your signal into the broader backbone of the internet.","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" This backbone consists of high-capacity fiber-optic lines and massive data centers—warehouses filled with thousands of servers that","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" store the websites and services we use every day.\n\n### Data Fragmentation: Packets and Binary\nComputers do not","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" send files like a single, solid block. If you were to send a high-resolution photo, it would be far","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" too large to move through the network in one piece. Instead, the internet uses a process called \"packet switching.\" ","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"\n\nYour data is broken down into tiny pieces called \"packets.\" Each packet contains a small portion of the actual data,","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" along with a \"header.\" This header acts like a shipping label, containing the sender's IP address, the destination","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"'s IP address, and instructions on how to reassemble the pieces. These packets are converted into binary—a series","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" of 1s and 0s—which are transmitted as electrical pulses (in copper), light pulses (in fiber","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"), or radio waves (in Wi-Fi).\n\n### The Post Office: IP and TCP\nOnce the packets are","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" created, they need a way to find their destination. This is where the Internet Protocol (IP) and the Transmission Control","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" Protocol (TCP) come in. Together, they are known as the TCP/IP suite.\n\nEvery device connected to","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" the internet has a unique identifier called an IP address (e.g., `192.168.","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"1.1` or the much longer IPv6 addresses). The **Internet Protocol (IP)** is responsible for addressing and","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" routing. It acts like the postal service, ensuring that each packet knows which \"house\" (device) it is headed","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" to. Routers—specialized computers that act as traffic controllers—examine the IP header of every packet and decide the","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" most efficient path for it to take across the global network.\n\nHowever, because packets can take different routes, they might","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" arrive out of order, or some might get lost entirely. This is where **TCP** steps in. TCP is the","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" \"manager\" of the connection. It numbers the packets so the receiving device knows how to put them back together. If","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" a packet goes missing, TCP detects the gap and sends a request to the sender to re-transmit that specific piece.","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" This ensures that the data you receive is complete and uncorrupted.\n\n### The Phonebook: DNS\nHumans are","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" not good at remembering strings of numbers like `142.250.190.46`.","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" We prefer names like `google.com`. To bridge this gap, the internet uses the Domain Name System (DNS).","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"\n\nWhen you type a URL into your browser, your computer first contacts a DNS resolver (usually managed by your ISP).","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" The resolver acts like a phonebook, looking up the human-readable domain name and returning the corresponding IP address. Once","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" your computer has the IP address, it can finally begin the process of establishing a direct connection with the server where the website","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" lives.\n\n### The Language of the Web: HTTP\nNow that the connection is established and the \"pipes\" are","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" laid, the devices need a common language to talk to one another. This is the Application Layer, and for the web","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":", that language is **HTTP (Hypertext Transfer Protocol)**.\n\nHTTP follows a simple request-response model. Your","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" browser (the client) sends an HTTP request to the server, saying something like, *\"GET /index.html.\"","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"* The server receives this request, finds the file, and sends back an HTTP response. This response includes a status code","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" (like `200 OK` if everything went well, or `404 Not Found` if the page","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" doesn't exist) and the actual content of the webpage.\n\nIn the modern era, we almost exclusively use **","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":"HTTPS**, where the \"S\" stands for \"Secure.\" This uses encryption (TLS/SSL) to scramble the data","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" being sent, ensuring that if someone were to intercept your packets, they would see only gibberish rather than your passwords","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" or credit card numbers.\n\n### Conclusion\nThe magic of the internet lies in its layers. A single click triggers a","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" cascade of events: a DNS lookup finds an address, TCP breaks data into packets, IP routes those packets through a global","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" web of fiber-optic cables, and HTTP translates the resulting data into the visual webpage you see on your screen. It","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

event: step.delta
data: {"index":1,"delta":{"text":" is a seamless, lightning-fast orchestration of physics, mathematics, and logic.","type":"text"},"event_type":"step.delta"}

